          <!-- ======================================================== decideBatch -->
          <section class="doc-section" id="decide-batch">
            <h2>API · decideBatch()</h2>
            <div class="api-sig"><span class="m">decideBatch</span>(
  requests: <span class="t">DecisionRequest</span>[],
  options?: <span class="t">DecideBatchOptions</span>
): Promise&lt;<span class="t">DecisionResponse</span>[]&gt;</div>

            <p>
              Runs decisions with bounded concurrency and preserves input order. The
              default concurrency is <code>4</code>. Each decision keeps its own timeout
              and retry budget.
            </p>

            <CodeBlock code={snippets.batchAdvanced} lang="ts" />

            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Option</th>
                    <th>Default</th>
                    <th>Description</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>concurrency</td>
                    <td><code>4</code></td>
                    <td>Positive integer. Caps simultaneous decisions.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>signal</td>
                    <td>—</td>
                    <td>Aborts every decision in the batch.</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <div class="callout warn">
              <span class="callout-icon">!</span>
              <div>
                <p>
                  A batch is fail-fast: the first rejected decision rejects the batch
                  promise. Wrap the call in <code>try/catch</code> if partial results are
                  acceptable, or call <code>decide()</code> directly for per-item error
                  handling.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- ============================================================= Errors -->
          <section class="doc-section" id="errors">
            <h2>Errors</h2>
            <p>
              Every error extends <code>XonusError</code> and exposes a stable
              <code>code</code>. API failures keep the original error as
              <code>cause</code>.
            </p>

            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Class</th>
                    <th>Code</th>
                    <th>When</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>XonusConfigError</td>
                    <td><code>XONUS_CONFIG</code></td>
                    <td>Missing key, invalid threshold, bad option count in config.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>XonusInputError</td>
                    <td><code>XONUS_INPUT</code></td>
                    <td>Empty question, duplicate options, fewer than 2 or more than 26 options.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>XonusApiError</td>
                    <td><code>XONUS_API</code></td>
                    <td>Provider or network failure. Includes <code>status</code> when known.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>XonusParseError</td>
                    <td><code>XONUS_PARSE</code></td>
                    <td>No usable option could be recovered from the response.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>XonusAbortError</td>
                    <td><code>XONUS_ABORTED</code></td>
                    <td>The caller's signal was aborted.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>XonusTimeoutError</td>
                    <td><code>XONUS_TIMEOUT</code></td>
                    <td>The <code>timeoutMs</code> deadline elapsed.</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <CodeBlock code={snippets.errorHandling} lang="ts" />

            <h3>Retries</h3>
            <p>
              Xonus retries HTTP 429, 408, 5xx, and common network failures with
              exponential backoff and jitter. Retries happen only around request
              startup: the fast request and the opening of the deliberation stream.
              Partial streams are never replayed.
            </p>
          </section>

          <!-- ============================================================= Tuning -->
          <section class="doc-section" id="tuning">
            <h2>Tuning</h2>
            <p>
              Two numbers control almost everything:
              <code>tau</code> (decisiveness) and <code>answerMassGate</code>
              (on-protocol probability). Tune them against a labelled sample, not by
              intuition alone.
            </p>

            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Goal</th>
                    <th>Settings</th>
                    <th>Trade-off</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Maximum precision</td>
                    <td><code>tau: 8</code>, <code>gate: 0.995</code></td>
                    <td>More human escalations; fewer confident mistakes.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Fewer escalations</td>
                    <td><code>tau: 4</code>, <code>gate: 0.95</code></td>
                    <td>More automation, less certainty per decision.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Cheapest mode</td>
                    <td><code>escalate: false</code>, <code>maxRetries: 1</code></td>
                    <td>One short request per decision; uncertain results are returned flagged.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Lowest latency</td>
                    <td><code>timeoutMs: 5000</code>, <code>maxOutputTokens: 256</code></td>
                    <td>Some decisions time out and need a fallback.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Harder reasoning (Gemini)</td>
                    <td><code>thinkingBudget: 256</code>, <code>maxOutputTokens: 1024</code></td>
                    <td>Higher cost and latency on escalated decisions.</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <h3>What to log</h3>
            <p>
              For each decision, record <code>decision</code>, <code>confident</code>,
              <code>logOdds</code>, <code>answerMass</code>, <code>passes</code>, and
              <code>latencyMs</code>. Plot the distributions of <code>logOdds</code> and
              <code>answerMass</code> against correctness on a labelled set. Pick the
              thresholds where the confident bucket meets your error budget.
            </p>

            <div class="callout warn">
              <span class="callout-icon">!</span>
              <div>
                <p>
                  Do not raise <code>answerMassGate</code> above what your
                  <code>topLogprobs</code> setting can observe. With only 20 candidates,
                  mass can look lower than it truly is. If a gate of <code>0.99</code>
                  rejects almost everything, lower the gate before increasing logprobs.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3>Threshold intuition</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><code>tau = 4.6</code> is roughly 100:1 odds.</li>
              <li><code>tau = 6.0</code> is roughly 403:1 odds.</li>
              <li><code>tau = 6.9</code> is roughly 1,000:1 odds.</li>
              <li><code>tau = 8.0</code> is roughly 2,981:1 odds.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>

