          <!-- ============================================================== Models -->
          <section class="doc-section" id="models">
            <h2>Models &amp; providers</h2>
            <p>
              Xonus works with any chat model that accepts a system instruction and a
              user prompt. Calibrated confidence additionally requires token log
              probabilities. Gemini, OpenAI-compatible APIs, and Anthropic are built in;
              anything else can use a custom adapter.
            </p>

            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Topic</th>
                    <th>Behavior</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Logprobs</td>
                    <td>
                      Requested with <code>topLogprobs</code> candidates (max 20) when the
                      provider supports them. If the model omits them, Xonus falls back
                      to text and reports zero confidence.
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Text-only models</td>
                    <td>
                      Set <code>logprobs: false</code> to skip the logprobs request. The
                      decision still resolves, but <code>confident</code> is always
                      <code>false</code>.
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Thinking</td>
                    <td>
                      Gemini only. Disabled by default (<code>thinkingBudget: 0</code>)
                      for predictable cost and clean decision tokens. Override through
                      <code>generationConfig</code>.
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Output limits</td>
                    <td>
                      Fast pass: 32 tokens max. Deliberation:
                      <code>maxOutputTokens</code> (default 512).
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Options</td>
                    <td>2–26, each non-empty and unique.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Streaming</td>
                    <td>
                      Deliberation is streamed by the built-in adapters so Xonus can abort
                      early. A custom provider may yield a single chunk.
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Cost</td>
                    <td>
                      One short request for confident decisions; two requests only when
                      escalation is needed. No sampling loop.
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <div class="callout">
              <span class="callout-icon">i</span>
              <div>
                <p>
                  Xonus caps <code>logOdds</code> at <code>99</code> so the value survives
                  <code>JSON.stringify</code>. A capped value means the runner-up was
                  effectively zero; it is not a precision limit on the model.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- ============================================================ Benchmark -->
          <section class="doc-section" id="benchmark">
            <h2>Benchmark proof</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              A 50-scenario empirical trial compared three rotation-sampling strategies
              under the same model, prompts, and option sets.
            </p>

            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Sampling strategy</th>
                    <th>Accuracy</th>
                    <th>Avg. rotations read</th>
                    <th>Total time (50 scenarios)</th>
                    <th>Relative speedup</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>van der Corput (AnyJev baseline)</td>
                    <td>90.0%</td>
                    <td>2.33</td>
                    <td>51.82s</td>
                    <td>1.00×</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Golden ratio (φ)</td>
                    <td>90.0%</td>
                    <td>2.42</td>
                    <td>51.80s</td>
                    <td>1.00×</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><strong>Active dynamic (Xonus)</strong></td>
                    <td><strong>90.0%</strong></td>
                    <td><strong>2.22</strong></td>
                    <td><strong>38.27s</strong></td>
                    <td><strong>1.35×</strong></td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <p>
              Accuracy is identical across all three strategies. Xonus saved 13.55
              seconds over the 50 scenarios — a 26.1% reduction in wall-clock time,
              computed from the totals above.
            </p>

            <h3>What the trial measures</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <strong>Accuracy</strong> — whether the chosen option matched the
                labelled answer.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Rotations read</strong> — how many candidate rotations the
                strategy inspected before committing.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Total time</strong> — wall-clock time to complete all 50
                scenarios, including model round trips.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <div class="callout">
              <span class="callout-icon">i</span>
              <div>
                <p>
                  Relative speedup is computed from the wall-clock totals, not copied from
                  a separate run. Always re-measure on your own model, prompts, and
                  latency profile before making a production decision.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
