          <!-- ============================================================== AnyJev -->
          <section class="doc-section" id="anyjev">
            <h2>Xonus vs. AnyJev</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              AnyJev is the foundational academic baseline. Xonus is the production
              engine built for real deployments: faster sampling, deterministic prompt
              construction, native application SDKs, and parallel prefill.
            </p>

            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Capability</th>
                    <th>AnyJev</th>
                    <th>Xonus</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Rotation selection</td>
                    <td>Static van der Corput sequence</td>
                    <td>Active dynamic sampling</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Prompt order</td>
                    <td>Input-order sensitive</td>
                    <td>Canonical pre-sort, then cyclic shift</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Packaging</td>
                    <td>PyTorch research codebase</td>
                    <td>TypeScript/Node SDK + Python integration path</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Prefill</td>
                    <td>Sequential round trips</td>
                    <td>Async parallel prefill</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <h3>1. Active dynamic sampling</h3>
            <p>
              AnyJev reads rotations from a static van der Corput sequence regardless of
              how close the options are. Xonus inspects the top two candidates and picks
              the next unread rotation that maximizes their spatial separation. In the
              50-scenario trial, that reduced the average from 2.33–2.42 rotations to
              2.22.
            </p>

            <h3>2. Canonical pre-sorting</h3>
            <p>
              Xonus sorts options alphabetically before the cyclic shift. The initial
              prompt set is invariant to the input array order, eliminating adjacency and
              position bias from the option list.
            </p>

            <h3>3. Native production SDKs</h3>
            <p>
              AnyJev shipped as an academic PyTorch codebase. Xonus exposes the decision
              protocol from TypeScript/Node.js on serverless and web platforms, with a
              Python integration path for vLLM/FastAPI services. The provider layer is
              shared, so the same options and thresholds work across runtimes.
            </p>

            <h3>4. Async parallel prefill</h3>
            <p>
              Xonus executes rotation queries asynchronously in parallel over cloud APIs
              such as Gemini Flash, collapsing wall-clock round trips into a single pass
              instead of waiting for each rotation sequentially.
            </p>
          </section>

